//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg"}},{"b":2,"n":"Tropical dry forest","full":"Tropical & Subtropical Dry Broadleaf Forests","col":"#99993a","share":2.9,"protected":8,"blurb":"Warm forests with a long dry season, when most trees drop their leaves to save water. They are among the most threatened forests, because their land is easy to farm.","animals":["Bengal tiger","Asian elephant","fossa","Verreaux's sifaka","Indian peafowl"],"plants":["teak","baobab","sandalwood"],"intact":"About two-fifths of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). One of the most heavily cleared forest biomes.","convertedPct":42,"intactPct":4,"where":"India, Mexico and Central America, Bolivia, Madagascar, Southeast Asia, Caribbean islands","photo":{"d":"photos/x187d68e70030e2c1.webp","by":"Timothy A. Gonsalves","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dry_Forest_Theppakadu_Mudumalai_Mar21_A7C_00505.jpg"}},{"b":3,"n":"Tropical pine forest","full":"Tropical & Subtropical Coniferous Forests","col":"#4f9c6a","share":0.5,"protected":12,"blurb":"Pine and oak forests on warm, mostly mountainous land. Mexico alone has more kinds of pine trees than any other country, and monarch butterflies spend the winter in its fir forests.","animals":["monarch butterfly","thick-billed parrot","volcano rabbit","Hispaniolan crossbill"],"plants":["Mexican pines","oaks","sacred fir (oyamel)","bromeliads"],"intact":"About a quarter of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines).","convertedPct":24,"intactPct":6,"where":"Mexico, Central America, Cuba and Hispaniola, Himalayan foothills, Philippines","photo":{"d":"photos/xf4b0f3d2cf46321d.webp","by":"Paco Olvera Monterd from Ciudad de Mexico D.F., México","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinus_hartwegii_forest.jpg"}},{"b":4,"n":"Temperate broadleaf forest","full":"Temperate Broadleaf & Mixed Forests","col":"#79b04f","share":9.5,"protected":10,"blurb":"Forests of oak, beech and maple with four seasons; many trees drop their leaves in autumn. People have farmed these lands for thousands of years, so much of the forest is gone.","animals":["red deer","wild boar","American black bear","red fox","European bison","tawny owl"],"plants":["oak","beech","maple","bluebells"],"intact":"About half of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). Much of it was cleared long ago for farms, towns and cities.","convertedPct":51,"intactPct":11,"where":"Eastern USA, Europe, China, Japan and Korea, Southeast Australia, New Zealand","photo":{"d":"photos/xb425777970952e9e.webp","by":"掬茶","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beech_forest_in_Nyuto_Onsenkyo_(2).jpg"}},{"b":5,"n":"Temperate conifer forest","full":"Temperate Conifer Forests","col":"#3c7c86","share":2.8,"protected":17,"blurb":"Evergreen forests of pine, fir, spruce and redwood in cool mountains and on rainy coasts. They include the tallest trees on Earth (coast redwoods) and the biggest (giant sequoias).","animals":["grizzly bear","elk","northern spotted owl","red squirrel","golden eagle"],"plants":["coast redwood","giant sequoia","Douglas fir","Scots pine"],"intact":"A small part (about 8%) of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). About 55% is still almost untouched.","convertedPct":8,"intactPct":57,"where":"Pacific Northwest, Rocky Mountains, Alps and Carpathians, Himalaya, Southeast USA pine woods","photo":{"d":"photos/xac9410f8753645a2.webp","by":"Dietmar Rabich","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banff_National_Park_(AB,_Canada),_Lake_Minnewanka_--_2022_--_2313.jpg"}},{"b":6,"n":"Boreal forest (taiga)","full":"Boreal Forests/Taiga","col":"#2b5d4f","share":11.6,"protected":9,"blurb":"The largest forest on Earth, a ring of spruce, pine and larch around the far north. Winters are long and very cold, and in many places the ground stays frozen all year.","animals":["moose","brown bear","grey wolf","Canada lynx","wolverine","sable"],"plants":["spruce","larch","birch","lingonberry"],"intact":"Almost none of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). About 90% is still almost untouched. One of the wildest biomes: most of it is still forest, though logging and fires are growing.","convertedPct":1,"intactPct":89,"where":"Canada, Alaska, Scandinavia, Russia (Siberia)","photo":{"d":"photos/x0125f854149bedbf.webp","by":"Ximonic (Simo Räsänen)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forest_near_Punainenhiekka_at_Pallasj%C3%A4rvi_in_Pallas-Yll%C3%A4stunturi_National_Park,_Kittil%C3%A4,_Lapland,_Finland,_2021_September.jpg"}},{"b":7,"n":"Tropical savanna","full":"Tropical & Subtropical Grasslands, Savannas & Shrublands","col":"#d9b24c","share":16.2,"protected":15,"blurb":"Warm grasslands dotted with trees, with a rainy season and a long dry season. They hold the biggest herds of large wild animals left on Earth.","animals":["African elephant","lion","giraffe","zebra","wildebeest","red kangaroo"],"plants":["acacia","baobab","elephant grass","red oat grass"],"intact":"About one-seventh of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). Large wild areas remain, but farms and cattle ranches are spreading, like in Brazil's Cerrado.","convertedPct":14,"intactPct":27,"where":"Africa, Brazil (Cerrado), Venezuela and Colombia (Llanos), Northern Australia, India","photo":{"d":"photos/x7731e4c13c6e46d6.webp","by":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serengeti-Landscape-2012.JPG"}},{"b":8,"n":"Temperate grassland","full":"Temperate Grasslands, Savannas & Shrublands","col":"#c8c46e","share":8.0,"protected":4,"blurb":"Wide, mostly treeless grasslands (prairies, steppes and pampas) with hot summers and cold winters. Their rich soil makes great farmland, so much of them has been ploughed.","animals":["American bison","pronghorn","prairie dog","saiga antelope","greater rhea","Przewalski's horse"],"plants":["feather grass","big bluestem","wild tulips","sunflower"],"intact":"About a third of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). One of the most converted and least protected biomes on Earth (Hoekstra et al. 2005); in the US tallgrass prairie about 99% is farmland (Dinerstein et al. 2017).","convertedPct":32,"intactPct":20,"where":"North American prairies, Eurasian steppe, Argentine pampas and Patagonia, Mongolia","photo":{"d":"photos/xf773011f54219bf7.webp","by":"User:Reservoirhill","lic":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tallgrass_Prairie_Nature_Preserve_in_Osage_County.jpg"}},{"b":9,"n":"Flooded grassland","full":"Flooded Grasslands & Savannas","col":"#4aa3a8","share":0.9,"protected":32,"blurb":"Grasslands and savannas that flood for part of the year, like the Pantanal, the Okavango Delta and the Everglades. They are magnets for birds, fish and big animals.","animals":["jaguar","capybara","hippopotamus","red lechwe","shoebill","American alligator"],"plants":["papyrus","sawgrass","water lilies","reeds"],"intact":"About one-seventh of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines).","convertedPct":17,"intactPct":16,"where":"Pantanal (South America), Okavango and Sudd (Africa), Florida Everglades, Mesopotamian Marshes, Rann of Kutch (India)","photo":{"d":"photos/3a6bcdca8263380b.webp","by":"Joachim Huber","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okavango_Delta,_Botswana3.jpg"}},{"b":10,"n":"Mountain grassland","full":"Montane Grasslands & Shrublands","col":"#a68fa0","share":3.7,"protected":25,"blurb":"High grasslands and shrublands above the tree line, with cold nights, strong sunshine and thin air. Many plants grow as low cushions or as giant rosettes.","animals":["snow leopard","wild yak","vicuña","Ethiopian wolf","gelada","Andean condor"],"plants":["giant lobelia","frailejón","edelweiss","Himalayan blue poppy"],"intact":"A small part (about 6%) of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). About 50% is still almost untouched.","convertedPct":6,"intactPct":51,"where":"Tibetan Plateau, Andes, East African mountains, Ethiopian Highlands, Himalaya, New Guinea","photo":{"d":"photos/xc3046b72d98e3eb3.webp","by":"Mario Andres Izquierdo Yañez UNAL","lic":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frailejones_en_el_P%C3%A1ramo_de_Ocet%C3%A1,_Boyac%C3%A1.jpg"}},{"b":11,"n":"Tundra","full":"Tundra","col":"#aebfca","share":6.4,"protected":8,"blurb":"Cold, treeless land near the poles where only the top layer of soil thaws in summer. Short plants, mosses and lichens feed caribou and geese in the brief summer.","animals":["polar bear","caribou (reindeer)","Arctic fox","snowy owl","muskox","lemming"],"plants":["cottongrass","Arctic willow","reindeer lichen","Arctic poppy"],"intact":"Almost none of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). Nearly all of it is still almost untouched. Very little has been changed by people, but the Arctic is warming fast.","convertedPct":0,"intactPct":98,"where":"Northern Canada and Alaska, Greenland's coasts, Northern Russia, Antarctic Peninsula and sub-Antarctic islands","photo":{"d":"photos/x1e5ae8fb6680390f.webp","by":"NPS Photo","lic":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fall_colors_of_the_arctic_tundra_on_a_bluebird_sky_day._(c51fb8df-1dd8-b71c-0753-1c57348df744).jpg"}},{"b":12,"n":"Mediterranean woodland","full":"Mediterranean Forests, Woodlands & Scrub","col":"#d98b4a","share":2.5,"protected":18,"blurb":"Lands with hot, dry summers and mild, rainy winters. Tough, fragrant shrubs and trees survive drought and fire, and these small regions are packed with plant species.","animals":["Iberian lynx","Cape sugarbird","California quail","quokka","Hermann's tortoise"],"plants":["olive tree","cork oak","protea","lavender"],"intact":"About two-fifths of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). Habitat loss is about 8 times greater than protection here, a 'biome crisis' (Hoekstra et al. 2005).","convertedPct":39,"intactPct":17,"where":"Lands around the Mediterranean Sea, California, Central Chile, South Africa's Cape, Southwest and South Australia","photo":{"d":"photos/x890e080b40c704db.webp","by":"T. Kebert","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macchia_TK_2022-04-15_1.jpg"}},{"b":13,"n":"Desert and dry shrubland","full":"Deserts & Xeric Shrublands","col":"#e8d6a4","share":19.9,"protected":6,"blurb":"Very dry lands that usually get less than 25 cm of rain a year. Deserts can be scorching hot like the Sahara or freezing in winter like the Gobi, and they cover about a fifth of the land.","animals":["dromedary camel","fennec fox","Arabian oryx","thorny devil","meerkat","Gila monster"],"plants":["saguaro cactus","date palm","welwitschia","Joshua tree"],"intact":"A small part (about 6%) of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). About 70% is still almost untouched.","convertedPct":6,"intactPct":69,"where":"Sahara, Arabia, Australian interior, Central Asia and Gobi, Kalahari and Namib, Southwest USA and Mexico, Atacama","photo":{"d":"photos/xd7bda74c793d2109.webp","by":"Thomas Fuhrmann","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erg_Chebbi_in_the_evening_light.jpg"}},{"b":14,"n":"Mangroves","full":"Mangroves","col":"#20c4b2","share":0.2,"protected":26,"blurb":"Salt-tolerant forests on muddy tropical coasts where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots shelter baby fish and protect coasts from storms and waves.","animals":["Bengal tiger","proboscis monkey","saltwater crocodile","mudskipper","West Indian manatee","fiddler crab"],"plants":["red mangrove","black mangrove","nipa palm"],"intact":"About two-fifths of it is heavily changed by people (farms, towns, roads, mines). (Mangroves are thin coastal strips, so this rough estimate includes nearby coasts.) Many mangroves have been cut for shrimp farms, rice and coastal building.","convertedPct":39,"intactPct":4,"where":"Sundarbans (India and Bangladesh), Southeast Asia, Brazil, West Africa, Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean, Northern Australia","photo":{"d":"photos/xf6ad63ee06f4c442.webp","by":"Armusaofficial","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mangrove_Islet.jpg"}},{"b":0,"n":"Rock and ice","full":"Rock and Ice","col":"#eef2f5","share":9.6,"protected":null,"blurb":"Land buried under ice sheets and glaciers, mostly in Antarctica and Greenland. Almost nothing grows here, but seals and penguins live along the icy coasts.","animals":["emperor penguin","Weddell seal","snow petrel"],"plants":["lichens","mosses"],"intact":"Mostly untouched ice sheets (not rated in Dinerstein et al. 2017).","where":"Antarctica, Greenland, Highest mountain glaciers","photo":{"d":"photos/x72a7c081a8bd7db2.webp","by":"Pedro Szekely from Los Angeles, USA","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antarctica_landscape_(52969317866).jpg"}}],"eco":[{"id":1,"n":"Albertine Rift montane forests","b":1,"r":"Afrotropic","ll":[28.77,-3.01],"km2":151504,"nnh":4,"nnhn":"Nature Imperiled","blurb":"Misty mountain forests along Africa's Great Rift. Mountain gorillas live here, in places like Uganda's Bwindi 'Impenetrable Forest'.","animals":["mountain gorilla","eastern chimpanzee","golden monkey","Rwenzori turaco"],"plants":["tree ferns","mountain bamboo","wild orchids"],"geom":{"type":"MultiPolygon","coordinates":[[[[30.3,-2.9],[30.4,-2.7],[30.2,-3.3],[30.2,-2.9],[30,-3.2],[29.9,-3.1],[30,-3.1],[30.1,-3],[29.9,-3],[30,-2.9],[29.9,-2.9],[30.2,-2.7],[30.3,-2.8],[30.3,-2.9]]],[[[29.8,-3.3],[29.9,-3.2],[29.8,-3.4],[29.8,-3.5],[29.9,-3.4],[30,-3.3],[30.1,-3.7],[30,-3.9],[29.9,-3.9],[29.9,-4.1],[29.8,-4.1],[29.7,-4.3],[29.7,-4.1],[29.6,-4.1],[29.6,-3.8],[29.4,-3.8],[29.3,-3.7],[29.3,-3.4],[29.1,-3.4],[29,-4.3]